AB  - This paper explores the application of large language models (LLMs) in
 automating the peer review process for academic papers, a critical ar
ea for enhancing the efficiency and consistency of scholarly publicati
on. We utilized GPT-4-0125 to automatically generate reviews for 20 pa
pers sourced from openreview.net and analyzed the AI-generated peer re
views for quality and effectiveness. The analysis includes a detailed 
assessment of the text properties of the reviews, such as sentiment, r
evealing that LLM-generated reviews tend to be more uniformly positive
 than their human-written counterparts. In addition, we conducted a us
er survey in which participants attempted to distinguish between AI-ge
nerated and human-written reviews. The survey results indicated a low 
correct identification rate, suggesting that participants often could 
not discern the origin of the review, thereby highlighting the potenti
al of LLMs to mimic human-like review qualities. However, the study al
so identifies limitations in LLM's performance, particularly concernin
g the variability in review quality, which appears to correlate with t
he model's vocabulary usage of the generated content.
